The [original incident page](/kb/incidents/4471) calls the date reports one known parser issue.
Its own computed table shows separate causes. Keep one ticket per cause.
This investigation uses the export dated 4 October 2026. It does not establish the current production state.

## Count reports by cause

<CauseTable ticket={4471} />

The export overlaps report pages. Deduplicate by report ID before counting.
Use the tenant directory, the platform's date order, the report channel, and the booking form.
The browser locale and intake tag are guesses, not the tenant's date convention.

The shared parser reads dates month first. It can swap the day and month for day-first tenants.
This group includes app and email reports, plus booking-v2 form reports.

The booking-v3 form displays `MM/DD/YYYY` to day-first tenants, then reads their input day first.
This is a separate hint defect. Fixing the shared parser alone does not fix it.
Keep these form reports in their own ticket.

The table also preserves the desk's one-day-move notes.
Those reports remain counted under parser by the component's classification.
Their descriptions need investigation: a one-day symptom alone does not prove a month/day swap.
Do not silently discard them or count them twice.

## When the desk received and merged reports

<QueueTimeline ticket={4471} />

Reports arrived before the large merge on 2 July. The merge spike does not mark when the incident began.
The received and merged series measure different events. The desk continued to receive reports after the merge.
A known label and a queued fix do not establish that the problem stopped.

## Replace subject-only triage

The [triage page](/kb/triage/dedupe) and [accepted decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject)
use matching subjects. Their review date is old enough to trigger the layout's stale notice.
The component below tests causes, not subjects.

<DedupeExplorer />

Different subjects can describe the same cause. Identical subjects can describe different causes.
Check tenant locale, channel, form, and the report body before merging.
Keep reports apart when the tenant or cause is unknown; matching subjects do not supply the missing evidence.
Preserve accepted decision 0001 as history. This page proposes replacing its subject-only rule with cause-based triage.

## Desk update: 5 October 2026

The [Challenge's engineering update](https://hq.mega.dev/challenges/what-it-shows) now closes the combined incident.
It records separate outcomes:

- Parser: fixed, with the description `fix(dates): parse dd/mm for eu locales`.
  Close the [parser ticket](https://github.com/CopyJosh/mega-s01e03-what-it-shows/issues/1) as completed.
- Hint: won't fix, because the booking-v3 form was retired.
  Close the [hint ticket](https://github.com/CopyJosh/mega-s01e03-what-it-shows/issues/2) as not planned.

These are the desk's reported engineering outcomes. This investigation did not independently test production.
Retiring a form is different from fixing its hint. Preserve that distinction in the ticket state and the record.
The historical export and its report counts remain unchanged.

## Follow-up

The separate tickets preserve the computed report counts, source evidence, and distinct closure reasons.
Investigate the one-day descriptions without inventing a third confirmed cause.
Re-read the table, timeline, and triage examples when the underlying data changes.
This page records an investigation, not a deployed fix.

